  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The value of $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between two consecutive tenths $a$ and $b,$ where $a<b.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\nand\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $10$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies.\nWe know that ${2}^{3}=8$ and ${3}^{3}=27$.\nSince $8<10<27$, we know that $2<\\sqrt[3]{10}<3$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $2$ and $3$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.0}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2.1}^{3} & =9.261 \\\\ {2.2}^{3} & =10.648 \\\\ {2.3}^{3} & =12.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $9.261<10<10.648$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{2.1}^{3}<10<{2.2}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n2.1<\\sqrt[3]{10}<2.2\n$$\n\nSo, $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ lies between $2.1$ and $2.2$. Since we are given that $a<b$, we have $a=2.1$ and $b=2.2$.",
